  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/EB2834">Theorem <span data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</span>.</a>colimit!homotopyhomotopy colimit Let $\calC $ and $\calI $ be fibrant simplicial categories and $F: \calI \rightarrow \calC $ a simplicial functor. Suppose given an object $C \in \calC $ and a compatible family of maps $\{  \eta _{I}: F(I) \rightarrow C \} _{I \in \calI }$. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The maps $\eta _ I$ exhibit $C$ as a homotopy colimit of the diagram $F$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
Let $f: \Nerve (\calI ) \rightarrow \Nerve (\calC )$ be the simplicial nerve of $F$, and $\overline{f}: \Nerve (\calI )^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \Nerve (\calC )$ the extension of $f$ determined by the maps $\{  \eta _ I \} $. Then $\overline{f}$ is a colimit diagram in $\Nerve (\calC )$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof of Theorem <a href="/tag/EB2834" data-tag="EB2834">4.2.4.1</a>:.</strong>
       Let $\mathbf{A}$ denote the category $\Set _{\Delta }^{\calC }$, endowed with the projective model structure. Let $j: \calC ^{op} \rightarrow \mathbf{A}$ denote the Yoneda embedding, and let $\calU $ denote the full subcategory of $\mathbf{A}$ spanned by those objects which are weakly equivalent to $j(C)$ for some $C \in \calC $, so that $j$ induces an equivalence of simplicial categories $\calC ^{op} \rightarrow \calU ^{\circ }$. Choose a trivial injective cofibration $j \circ F \rightarrow F'$, where $F'$ is a injectively fibrant object of $\mathbf{A}^{\calI ^{op}}$. Let $f': \Nerve (\calI )^{op} \rightarrow \Nerve ( \calU ^{\circ } )$ be the nerve of $F'$, and let $C' = j(C)$, so that the maps $\{  \eta _{I}: F(I) \rightarrow C \} _{I \in \calI }$ induce a natural transformation $\alpha : \delta (C') \rightarrow f'$, where $\delta : \Nerve ( \calU ^{\circ }) \rightarrow \Fun ( \Nerve (\calI )^{op}, \Nerve (\calU ^{\circ }) )$ denotes the diagonal embedding. In view of Lemma <a href="/tag/CE8F7D" data-tag="CE8F7D">4.2.4.3</a>, condition $(1)$ admits the following reformulation: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1')$"><p>
For every object $A \in \calU ^{\circ }$, composition with $\alpha $ induces a homotopy equivalence 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{ \Nerve (\calU ^{\circ }) }( A,C') \rightarrow \bHom _{ \Fun ( \Nerve (\calI )^{op}, \Nerve (\calU ^{\circ })) }( \delta (A),f' ). \]
</div>
</li>
</ul>
    <p>
      Using Proposition <a href="/tag/AAC4A7" data-tag="AAC4A7">4.2.4.4</a>, we can reformulate this condition again: 
    <p>
      <ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1'')$"><p>
For every object $A \in \calU ^{\circ }$, the canonical map 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{ \mathbf{A}}( A,C') \rightarrow \bHom _{ \mathbf{A}^{ \calI ^{op}}}( \delta '(A), F') \]
</div>
<p>
 is a homotopy equivalence, where $\delta ': \mathbf{A}\rightarrow \mathbf{A}^{\calI ^{op}}$ denotes the diagonal embedding. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
    <p>
      Let $B \in \mathbf{A}$ be a limit of the diagram $F'$, so we have a canonical map $\beta : C' \rightarrow B$ between fibrant objects of $\mathbf{A}$. Condition $(2)$ is equivalent to the assertion that $\beta $ is a weak equivalence in $\mathbf{A}$, while condition $(1'')$ is equivalent to the assertion that composition with $\beta $ induces a homotopy equivalence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}(A, C') \rightarrow \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}(A, B) \]
</div>
    <p>
       for each $A \in \calU ^{\circ }$. The implication $(2) \Rightarrow (1'')$ is clear. Conversely, suppose that $(1'')$ is satisfied. For each $X \in \calC $, the object $j(X)$ belongs to $\calU ^{\circ }$, so that $\beta $ induces a homotopy equivalence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  C'(X) \simeq \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}( j(X), C') \rightarrow \bHom _{\mathbf{A}}( j(X), B) \simeq B(X). \]
</div>
    <p>
       It follows that $\beta $ is a weak equivalence in $\mathbf{A}$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>